I think it would be very cool to be playing a game of nethack and have
your character bump into someone else's, chat for a bit, and then
continue on your quests, or even team up.  Like on the muds.

However, I agree with the people who said it would be nearly
impossible to implement this without sacrificing a whole whopper load
of playability.  There are many problems with the idea of a
multi-player nethack, and you can't ignore them!

1) TIMING - as people have pointed out, Nethack will be about as fast
as a MUD at best.  This is because each person has to be allowed a
fair opportunity to type in a command.  Well, in muds, it's not that
important if you take a few seconds to type in a command, since then
only time that delay coudl really hurt you is when you're fighting
monsters.  To fight monsters, you have to be in the same room as them.
Well, nethack is different.  Monsters move every time you move.  There
are no "rooms" defined in Nethack that are rigidly defined as those in
MUDS.
   Also, it's a bitch, like people said, if someone get's paralyzed.
They get to wack off for half an hour while their character just
stands their.  What a fun game!
   And say you enter a long hallway.  Do you have to hit LEFT ARROW
each time you want to move a space?  Do your keypresses get stored in
a buffer somewhere?  These are details.  Yes, they are details.  The
dev team has to worry about these details even if we don't.
   So timing is gonna be a terrible fun-killer.  Someone mentioned
having a max of 4 players at a time, which is a reasonable idea, but
it still changes Nethack severely.

2) WHERE WILL WE PLAY IT?
   Well, it looks like MultiHack would have to be played on a UNIX
machine somewhere.  No more PC, since most PC's are limited to one
modem connection at a time (we're not talking max capabilities here,
we are talking about practicality)
   Or MultiHack would appear on a few BBS's as doors.  Boring.  BBS's
are unreliable anyway.
   I'd prefer to actually be able to play the next version of NETHACK
on my PC.  So Multi-user-ality isn' very high in priority for me,
unless some clever system is developed that allows inter-player
communication (and maybe a little interaction) without killing the
game's fun.

3) WHAT COULD THE DEV TEAM HAVE BEEN THINKING UP IN THE MEANTIME?
   While the Dev team devoted all their efforts into putting together
a MultiHack attempt, they could have been improving on the game we
already have instead of trying to make a new game.  They could be
adding new dungeons and quests, new characters, new towns, more NPC's,
and tons of neat new features that make the game richer and richer.
   just look at Omega.  It has a tons of GREAT ideas, like the
different caves being in the goblin mines and the sewer systems, etc.
And the fighting arena...  And walking around out-of-doors.  All that
was so neat when I first saw it!  And Omega is only a one-player-game.
Just imagine what Nethack-meets-omega would be like!  All the best
features of Nethack (its playabilty is a strongpoint) and all the best
featues of omega (its complexity and originiality).  Well, I'm sure
such a game would cease to be Nethack per se, but at least the game
would be conceptually the same!
   Making something like Nethack multi-player is a pretty good dream,
but regretfully little more.  i think there are richer soils to be
trod if we'd just stop eyeing our neighbor's meadow.

